What this does and does not license:
 - Power is computed with the observed rho as the true rho, the usual plug-in, and
   it is generous: the true value sits inside an interval this file reproduces, so
   real power against a weaker truth is lower than printed.
 - The ceiling is a harder limit than power. A scorer that emits 4 values across 33
   candidates cannot express a perfect ranking of them, so the loss is in the
   scorer's resolution, not in the number of candidates or the calibration.
 - More candidates sharpen agreement with the rubric, not agreement with hiring.
   The labels are generated from the criteria the pipeline encodes, so n = 250
   measures the same circularity more precisely.
 - Rows with n > 33 are bootstrap resamples of the 33 real candidates, scores and
   labels drawn on the same indices so a candidate stays one candidate. They repeat
   candidates and inherit their quirks, so they size the design's power rather than
   predicting a new experiment's result.
 - A null at n = 33 is not a proof of no effect. It is a statement that this
   design, on these labels, could not have told the difference apart.
